{"id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch07_CompleteManifoldsHopfRinowHadamard#def:dc-ch7-2-4","title":"def:dc-ch7-2-4","path":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch07_CompleteManifoldsHopfRinowHadamard.md","line":53,"body":"","kind":"definition","status":"not_started","tex":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch07_CompleteManifoldsHopfRinowHadamard.tex#def:dc-ch7-2-4","uses":["[[references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:dc-ch1-2-9]]","[[references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods#def:dc-ch3-3-1]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch07_CompleteManifoldsHopfRinowHadamard.tex","label":"def:dc-ch7-2-4","line":73,"source":"\\begin{definition}[distance]\n\n  \\label{def:dc-ch7-2-4}\n\n  \\uses{def:dc-ch1-2-9, def:dc-ch3-3-1}\n\n  \\dcref{ch7:2.4}\nThe \\emph{distance} $d(p,q)$ is defined by $d(p,q)=$ infimum of the lengths of all\n  curves $f_{p,q}$, where $f_{p,q}$ is a piecewise differentiable curve joining $p$\n  to $q$. Equivalently, one may take the infimum over curves that are smooth on\n  the subintervals of a finite partition.\n\\end{definition}\n\\begin{proof}\n\\sketch Additivity of length and the triangle inequality give one inequality.\n  Conversely, subdivide a $C^1$ path into small chart windows and replace each\n  piece by the pullback of a straight segment. Continuity of the chart Gram\n  forms makes the polygonal lengths converge to the original length.\n\\end{proof}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Definition</div></section>[distance] <p></p><span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-6465663a64632d6368372d322d34\"></span> <p></p><a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh01_RiemannianMetrics%23def%3Adc-ch1-2-9\">↗ def:dc-ch1-2-9</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FDoCarmo%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods%23def%3Adc-ch3-3-1\">↗ def:dc-ch3-3-1</a> <p></p><span class=\"tex-source-references\" role=\"list\" style=\"display:block\"><span class=\"tex-dcref\" role=\"listitem\" style=\"display:block\">1. ch7:2.4</span></span>The <em>distance</em> <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$d(p,q)$\">$d(p,q)$</span> is defined by <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$d(p,q)=$\">$d(p,q)=$</span> infimum of the lengths of all curves <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$f_{p,q}$\">$f_{p,q}$</span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$f_{p,q}$\">$f_{p,q}$</span> is a piecewise differentiable curve joining <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$p$\">$p$</span> to <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$q$\">$q$</span>. Equivalently, one may take the infimum over curves that are smooth on the subintervals of a finite partition.  <section class=\"theorem proof\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Proof</div><p>\\sketch Additivity of length and the triangle inequality give one inequality. Conversely, subdivide a <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$C^1$\">$C^1$</span> path into small chart windows and replace each piece by the pullback of a straight segment. Continuity of the chart Gram forms makes the polygonal lengths converge to the original length. </p><span class=\"qed\" aria-label=\"证毕\" style=\"display:block;text-align:right\">□</span></section>","warnings":["部分 TeX 排版细节未完整还原（已保留可读内容）: sketch"]},"previous":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch07_CompleteManifoldsHopfRinowHadamard#prop:dc-ch7-2-3","next":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch07_CompleteManifoldsHopfRinowHadamard#lem:dc-ch7-2-4-finite","relations":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:dc-ch1-2-9","title":"def:dc-ch1-2-9","label":"def:dc-ch1-2-9"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods#def:dc-ch3-3-1","title":"def:dc-ch3-3-1","label":"def:dc-ch3-3-1"}],"backlinks":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_GeodesicsConvexNeighborhoods#lem:dc-ch3-3-9-corner","title":"lem:dc-ch3-3-9-corner","label":"lem:dc-ch3-3-9-corner"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch07_CompleteManifoldsHopfRinowHadamard#lem:dc-ch7-2-4-finite","title":"lem:dc-ch7-2-4-finite","label":"lem:dc-ch7-2-4-finite"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch07_CompleteManifoldsHopfRinowHadamard#lem:dc-ch7-2-5-chart","title":"lem:dc-ch7-2-5-chart","label":"lem:dc-ch7-2-5-chart"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch07_CompleteManifoldsHopfRinowHadamard#lem:dc-ch7-2-5-pos","title":"lem:dc-ch7-2-5-pos","label":"lem:dc-ch7-2-5-pos"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch07_CompleteManifoldsHopfRinowHadamard#prop:dc-ch7-2-5","title":"prop:dc-ch7-2-5","label":"prop:dc-ch7-2-5"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch07_CompleteManifoldsHopfRinowHadamard#prop:dc-ch7-2-6","title":"prop:dc-ch7-2-6","label":"prop:dc-ch7-2-6"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch07_CompleteManifoldsHopfRinowHadamard#cor:dc-ch7-2-7","title":"cor:dc-ch7-2-7","label":"cor:dc-ch7-2-7"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch07_CompleteManifoldsHopfRinowHadamard#thm:dc-ch7-2-8","title":"thm:dc-ch7-2-8","label":"thm:dc-ch7-2-8"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch07_CompleteManifoldsHopfRinowHadamard#lem:dc-ch7-2-8-lipschitz","title":"lem:dc-ch7-2-8-lipschitz","label":"lem:dc-ch7-2-8-lipschitz"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/DoCarmo/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch07_CompleteManifoldsHopfRinowHadamard#lem:dc-ch7-2-8-c-to-d","title":"lem:dc-ch7-2-8-c-to-d","label":"lem:dc-ch7-2-8-c-